"Nielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0001870825000106)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Nielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists ... There is an infinite family of closed symplectic 4–manifolds, where each symplectic 4–manifold contains a Lagrangian projective plane R, such that any odd power of is non-trivial even in .\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: March 2025\n\nArticle: 110112\n\nVolume: Volume 463\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Nielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Mihail Arabadji, R. İnanç Baykur\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2025.110112\n\nAccess through your organizationView Open ManuscriptPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (2)\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe demonstrate the existence of numerous non-spin 4–manifolds for which the smooth Nielsen realization problem fails; namely, there exist finite subgroups of their mapping class groups that cannot be realized by any group of diffeomorphisms. This extends and complements recent results for spin 4–manifolds. Our examples span virtually all possible intersection forms, both even and odd, indefinite and definite, and include many irreducible 4–manifolds. To derive these examples, we study multi-twists, projective twists, and multi-reflections, which are all mapping classes supported around collections of embedded spheres and projective planes. Our obstructions to Nielsen realization are based on the work of Konno. We investigate projective twists in further detail, and notably, employ them to show that, for many closed symplectic 4–manifolds, the symplectic Torelli group is not generated by squared Dehn twists.\n\n## MSC\n\nprimary,\n\n57R50\n\n;\n\nsecondary,\n\n57K43\n\n,\n\n57M60\n\n## Keywords\n\n4-manifold\n\n;\n\nNielsen realization\n\n;\n\nProjective twist\n\n;\n\nMapping class group\n\n## Introduction\n\nLet X be a closed oriented smooth 4–manifold and let be its smooth mapping class group, which consists of smooth isotopy classes of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms. The Nielsen realization problem [22], which famously has an affirmative answer for surfaces [12], asks if the natural surjection has a section over any finite subgroup of .\n\nRecent results by Kronheimer–Mrowka [17], Baraglia–Konno [4], Lin [21], Farb-Looijenga [7] and Konno [13] demonstrated that the Nielsen realization fails for many spin 4–manifolds. Along with the classical examples of Raymond and Scott [24], which are nilmanifolds constructed as the mapping tori of the 3–torus, all the examples of 4–manifolds for which the Nielsen realization fails admit spin structures, and thus have even intersection forms. On the other hand, Lee recently showed that many finite subgroups of the topological mapping class group of small rational surfaces are realized by finite groups of diffeomorphisms [18]. These cumulatively yield the question of to what extend the Nielsen realization is governed by the intersection form; e.g. [14, Problem 5.2].\n\nThe main goal of this note is to show that the failure of the Nielsen realization problem is virtually independent of the intersection form.\n\nTheorem A\n\nGiven any non-zero integer s, for all but finitely many indefinite unimodular integral quadratic form Q with signature , there is a closed oriented smooth non-spin 4–manifold X with finite fundamental group, intersection form , and an order two subgroup of that does not lift to . The examples include irreducible 4–manifolds realizing infinitely many intersection forms.\n\nOur results are of slightly different flavor in the definite case:\n\nTheorem B\n\nFor any standard definite unimodular integral quadratic form Q with rank at least five, there is a closed oriented smooth 4–manifold X with finite fundamental group, intersection form , and an order two subgroup of which does not lift to .\n\nIn the proofs of these theorems, we employ a small variety of mapping classes which are compactly supported around certain embedded spheres and projective planes in an oriented 4–manifold. In Theorem A, we use multi-twists, simultaneous Dehn twists along a collection of disjoint spheres with normal Euler numbers ±2, whereas in Theorem B, we use multi-reflections along a collection of disjoint spheres with normal Euler numbers ±1. The obstructions to the realizability of these mapping classes we consider are derived from the inspiring work of Konno in [13], by carefully cooking up our examples so that their universal covers are spin 4–manifolds subject to the constraints from involutive Seiberg-Witten theory.\n\nDifferent examples of non-spin 4–manifolds for which the Nielsen realization fails were given very recently by Konno, Miyazawa and Taniguchi [15]. The authors showed that for certain non-minimal 4–manifolds, a reflection along a 2–sphere with normal Euler number −1, which is an involution locally modeled as complex conjugation around the exceptional sphere, constitutes an order two mapping class that does not lift. Akin to Konno's earlier work in [13], the constraints given in [15] allow us to build further non-liftable examples by studying multi-reflections, which are simultaneous reflections along a collection of disjoint 2–spheres with normal Euler numbers ±1. We have thus opted to revise our earlier results in the definite case to include these examples, which both simplified our proofs and improved the overall statement of Theorem B.\n\nMoreover, we delve into a study projective twists, which are analogs of Dehn twists performed along projective planes with normal Euler number ±1 and are smooth mapping classes that may be of independent interest. We observe that, unlike the Dehn twist along a sphere [25], locally the projective twist has infinite order; see Proposition 7. We also show that a projective twist along R in X may or may not be trivial in , depending on the topology of the pair ; see Proposition 9 and Remark 10. Although we cannot determine the order of projective twists in general, we propose them as another potential source of non-liftable subgroups of the smooth mapping class groups of 4–manifolds; in particular, we present a curious pair where X is a definite irreducible 4–manifold; see Remark 24.\n\nA noteworthy outcome of our investigation of projective twists is a negative answer to the following question attributed to Donaldson [27], [19]: Is the symplectic Torelli mapping class group of a closed symplectic 4–manifold generated by squared Dehn twists along Lagrangian spheres? Notably, the answer is affirmative for positive rational symplectic surfaces [19]; however, we show that this is not the case in general:\n\nTheorem C\n\nThere is an infinite family of closed symplectic 4–manifolds, where each symplectic 4–manifold contains a Lagrangian projective plane R, such that any odd power of is non-trivial even in . It follows that the symplectic Torelli group is not generated by squared Dehn twists, even in . In these examples, we can take X with finite and of any non-negative symplectic Kodaira dimension.\n\nAs it is the case in all the examples we produce in this paper, symplectic 4–manifolds in Theorem C are not simply connected, but can be taken with .\n\nConventions and outline of the article. All the manifolds and maps we consider in this article are smooth unless explicitly stated otherwise. For a compact connected oriented manifold X, we denote by the group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of X, and when the boundary , we assume they restrict to identity in a neighborhood of ∂X. We denote by the (smooth) mapping class group of X. Given a symplectic form on ω on X, let be the subgroup of stabilizing the form ω. We call the subgroup of the symplectic mapping class group that consists of elements that act trivially on the symplectic Torelli group, and denote it by .\n\nWe provide all the formal definitions and discuss the properties of multi-twists, projective twists and multi-reflections in Section 2. Obstructions coming from involutive Seiberg-Witten theory are gathered in Section 3. In Sections 4, 5 and 6, we prove Theorem A, Theorem B, Theorem C, respectively. Their proofs are going to involve a potpourri of smooth, symplectic and complex constructions in real dimension four (albeit with mainly smooth outcomes) to produce the desired examples of 4–manifolds with particular collections of spheres and projective planes in them and particular universal finite covers.\n\n\n### Multi-twists, projective twists and multi-reflections\n\nHere we are going to define the three types of mapping classes that are of interest to us in this article and review their basic properties.\n\n### Obstructions from Seiberg-Witten theory\n\nIn Theorem 17, Theorem 21 below we are going to list some constraints on lifting subgroups generated by multi-twists and multi-reflections, respectively. These results and their proofs are direct generalizations of the ones given by Konno in [13] and Konno, Miyazawa and Taniguchi in [15] using different flavors of involutive Seiberg-Witten theory.\n\nLemma 15\n\nLet X be a closed oriented spin 4–manifold with and let be a disjoint union of embedded spheres {S}_{i}, where each one of the first {k}_{+}\n\n### Proof of Theorem A\n\nIn this section, we present the non-spin examples with indefinite intersection forms promised in Theorem A. All the mapping classes we are going to involve are multi-twists.\n\nProof of Theorem A\n\nWe deal with the odd and even intersection forms separately.\n\nOdd forms: Our building block is a closed oriented smooth 4–manifold T with finite fundamental group, signature and spin universal (finite) cover \\tilde{T}; the existence of such a 4–manifold is proved by Teichner in [29]. We are going to thus refer to T as\n\n### Proof of Theorem B\n\nOne of the main building block we are going to use here is the Hitchin manifold H, which is a closed oriented Einstein manifold that arises as a quotient of a complex Enriques surface by a free anti-holomorphic involution [11].\n\nLemma 23\n\nThe Hitchin manifold H satisfies the following:\n\n  * (a)\n\nH is irreducible and has negative-definite intersection form .\n\n  * (b)\n\nH contains an essential projective plane R with normal Euler number −1.\n\nProof\n\n(a) The algebraic topology of the closed oriented smooth 4–manifold H is easily\n\n### Proof of Theorem C\n\nIn this final section, we show that projective twists along essential Lagrangian projective planes give rise to examples of elements of the symplectic Torelli group that cannot be expressed as a product of squared Dehn twists along Lagrangian spheres.\n\nProof of Theorem C\n\nStart with a complex elliptic surface with two nodal fibers. Symplectically blowing-up at one of the two nodes and then rationally blowing down the resulting -curve we get a new symplectic 4–manifold {Y}^{\\prime }. Repeating this at the other node,\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists (https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.10399)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Title: Nielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists ... Our examples span virtually all possible intersection forms, both even and odd, indefinite and definite, and include many irreducible 4-manifolds.\nTitle: Nielsen realization in dimension four and projective twists\nAuthors: Mihail Arabadji, R. Inanc Baykur\nDate: Thu Apr 20 15:36:26 2023\n\nWe demonstrate the existence of numerous non-spin 4-manifolds for which the smooth Nielsen realization problem fails; namely, there exist finite subgroups of their mapping class groups that cannot be realized by any group of diffeomorphisms. This extends and complements recent