folds $\\#_n \\mathbb{CP}^2 (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/405371620_Homological_Nielsen_realization_for_the_manifolds_n_mathbbCP2)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Given a smooth, oriented, simply-connected 4-manifold M, the homological Nielsen realization problem asks: when does a finite group of isometries $G\\leq O(H_2(M;\\mathbb{Z}))$ preserving the intersection form lift isomorphically to a finite group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms? ... Even though every isometry of $H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z})$ is induced by some orientation-preserving diffeomorphism, not necessarily of finite order, we show that Nielsen realization is sparse: as $n\\to\\infty$, a random subgroup of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$ is asymptotically almost never realizable in $\\mathrm{Diff}^+(M_n)$; the same is true for random odd order elements of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$.\nPreprint\n\n# Homological Nielsen realization for the manifolds $\\#_n \\mathbb{CP}^2\n\n  * May 2026\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2605.27537\n\nAuthors:\n\nEthan Pesikoff\n\nEthan Pesikoff\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nGiven a smooth, oriented, simply-connected 4-manifold M, the homological Nielsen realization problem asks: when does a finite group of isometries $G\\leq O(H_2(M;\\mathbb{Z}))$ preserving the intersection form lift isomorphically to a finite group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms? We study this question for the smooth, positive-definite 4-manifolds $M_n:=\\#_n\\mathbb{CP}^2$. Even though every isometry of $H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z})$ is induced by some orientation-preserving diffeomorphism, not necessarily of finite order, we show that Nielsen realization is sparse: as $n\\to\\infty$, a random subgroup of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$ is asymptotically almost never realizable in $\\mathrm{Diff}^+(M_n)$; the same is true for random odd order elements of $O(H_2(M_n;\\mathbb{Z}))$. We present both positive realization results in certain cases and a range of obstructions to realization in other cases. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Dehn twists and the Nielsen realization problem for spin 4-manifolds (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/359411344_Dehn_twists_and_the_Nielsen_realization_problem_for_spin_4-manifolds)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.6 years ago; Crawled: last month; are of order 2, and say that ι is of odd type if the lifts are of order 4. ... Let X be an oriented closed smooth 4-manifold and s be a spin struc-\nare of order 2, and say that ι is of odd type if the lifts are of order 4. When the\n\nﬁxed-point set X ι is non-empty, the codimension of all components of X ι are the\n\nsame, which is either 4 or 2, and the parity of ι determines which of them arises:\n\nX ι is of codimention-4 if ι is of even type, and X ι is of codimention-2 if ι is of odd\n\ntype ([1, Proposition 8.46], see also [26]).\n\nLemma 2.1. Let X be an oriented closed smooth 4-manifold and s be a spin struc-\n\nture on X. Let ι:X→X be an orientation-preserving diﬀeomorphism of order\n\n2, and suppose that ι preserves (the isomorphism class of) s and is of even type.\n\nThen we have σ ι(X) = σ(X)/2.\n\nProof. By Hirzebruch’s signature theorem (for example, [14]), σ ι(X) can be ob-\n\ntained by adding σ(X)/2 to contributions from ﬁxed surfaces of ι. (Note that, for a\n\ngeneral involution, the contribution from isolated ﬁxed points are zero.) However,\n\nX ι does not contain surfaces since ι is even. \n\nA key input to this paper is the following 10/8-type constraint on odd smooth\n\ninvolutions, proved by Y. Kato [15] using Seiberg–Witten theory:\n\nTheorem 2.2 ([15, Theorem 2.3]).Let (X, s)be a smooth closed oriented spin\n\n4-manifold. Let ι:X→X be a smooth orientation-preserving involution, and\n\nsuppose that ι preserves s and is of odd type. Then we have\n\n−σ(X)\n\n16 ≤b+(X)−b ι\n\n+(X).(2)\n\n4 HOKUTO KONNO\n\nRemark 2.3.In the notation of [15], Theorem 2.2 is stated using a quantity